│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ symmetry_weighted_voting_ansatz                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ This edit enhances the ansatz by focusing on the 'voting dominance' of the                │
│                          │ cross/circle/draw observables.                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Strategic Entanglement**: Instead of using CRZ (which is a conditional phase shift), │
│                          │ I use **CNOT** gates. CNOTs are more powerful at generating maximal entanglement between  │
│                          │ the center (wire 8) and the ring, which is crucial since the 'draw' class depends solely  │
│                          │ on wire 8's Z-expectation value.                                                          │
│                          │ 2. **Parametric Diversity**: I replace the shared `ry_out_periphery` with individual      │
│                          │ parameters for corners vs edges. In Tic-Tac-Toe, corners and edges are mathematically     │
│                          │ distinct (corners are part of 3 win-lines, edges only 2), so separating them allows the   │
│                          │ circuit to weight their contributions to the "cross" and "circle" sums more effectively.  │
│                          │ 3. **Axis Mixing**: I keep the RY-RZ-RX-RY structure but ensure that the CNOTs are        │
│                          │ sandwiched between non-commutation rotations to maximize the expressivity of the SU(2)    │
│                          │ block while maintaining symmetry-based parameter sharing.                                 │
